# --- Publish boundary ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `export-ignore` keeps a path out of `git archive`, and `git archive HEAD` is exactly how
# ~/.gittools/publish.sh builds the tree it pushes. So everything marked here stays tracked and
# versioned locally while never reaching the public repository -- which is the right shape for
# material that is real work but is nobody else's business.
#
# Verify what would actually ship with:
#     git archive HEAD | tar -t
#
# Note the `tools/**` glob below rather than `tools/`. Marking a *directory* export-ignore makes
# git archive skip it without descending, so the three `-export-ignore` re-inclusions under it are
# never even consulted and the files silently do not ship. The `**` form marks the files instead,
# git still walks in, and the exceptions take effect. Checked both ways; do not "simplify" it back.
#
# docs/ is the porting process, not the mod: lane charters, escalation inboxes, play reports, a
# running record of what broke and what it taught. Several carry absolute paths from this machine
# and one carries an email address, so the boundary is a leak guard as well as an editorial one.
docs/** export-ignore

# The analysis tools read from _reference/, which is gitignored upstream clones of BOP and a
# decompiled BTA -- so they cannot run against a clone of the public repo and would only mislead.
# The three exceptions below are the asset-provenance machinery, and those ARE published on
# purpose: they are how the licence position is checked, and a claim about what is in the jar is
# worth more when the thing that verifies it is in the open.
tools/** export-ignore
tools/classify_asset_provenance.py -export-ignore
tools/gen_asset_bridge_manifest.py -export-ignore
tools/strip_bridged_assets.py -export-ignore

# A local dev convenience that names this machine's cmd.exe configuration and the agent harness.
run-client.bat export-ignore
